#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364841 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "51d599af-36d8-560c-940b-0d2c5e18e818" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 5 \\\\ 39 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364843} {:answer/id #uuid "baee1d1b-d00c-5acb-94f6-f0427a62b462" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-39 & 8 \\\\ 5 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364844} {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e1902-07ed-50fd-88a1-9411422c1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364845} {:answer/id #uuid "0ac9e8ea-746e-5628-8717-3115f17fe124" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 39 \\\\ 8 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364846} {:answer/id #uuid "88643928-a221-5d54-9574-72c2f8cd9f7d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -1 \\\\ 9 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364847}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ac6e1902-07ed-50fd-88a1-9411422c1346" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364845} :answer-field/id #uuid "21999da2-cdb2-5afa-a867-f7b96c23e2f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364842}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "81e8f58d-3db1-5493-9653-beb052e8ad62" :question/math-academy-id "q-140482" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{4\\cdot (-1)-(-1)\\cdot 5}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 5 \\\\ -1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-2 & 1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}7 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & -5 \\\\ 1 & 4\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-8 & -39 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129569 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "b0dcc57b-b70d-5099-8655-1c525b6e5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127209} {:answer/id #uuid "d3a8c213-e37c-5cae-a4a0-9928b2b5de18" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127210} {:answer/id #uuid "db7f8e7c-701f-5122-ac01-625e0e9c905d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127211} {:answer/id #uuid "3343d4eb-e38d-561c-81a6-5e7c140fd645" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127212} {:answer/id #uuid "6b977d7f-c5f1-5444-a63d-5ba017027f85" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b0dcc57b-b70d-5099-8655-1c525b6e5b7a" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127209} :answer-field/id #uuid "3aa05285-18b6-579f-8c04-dbac65b8b645" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186127208}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "ba13dd29-8b1d-5a17-aa9e-1e011399048f" :question/math-academy-id "q-38096" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2\\cdot (-1)-1\\cdot (-1)}[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -5 \\\\ 2 & -8\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}0 & -2 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & -1 \\\\ 1 & -2\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-2 & 4 \\\\ 2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364848 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "86028fae-a119-5194-975d-a6d9fad6faf5" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Given Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ef43e3a-cda9-5020-acb4-21ce9d200d01" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}9 & 6 \\\\ -22 & -15\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364850} {:answer/id #uuid "bd3ac22a-01b8-5370-b0bc-d5302f7a8d03" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 5 & 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364851} {:answer/id #uuid "0f1f3e4f-30fa-5a82-8dea-e5ef25816576" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364852} {:answer/id #uuid "6e424319-ef94-5dfa-aff2-86acb84a4024" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364853} {:answer/id #uuid "6e5ab615-3c39-57ba-a8a8-01ef20a332ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364854}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6e5ab615-3c39-57ba-a8a8-01ef20a332ad" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364854} :answer-field/id #uuid "7ce533f6-294a-5e6a-aeaa-9556bcfe8b5e" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364849}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045464 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "99a47024-381b-53ae-b4e4-9c86f82427cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-38809" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 8\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]},\\,C={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the bases $B$ and $C$ of ${R}^{2},$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$, shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1},\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nFirst, let's denote the vectors of $B$ and $C$ as ${{b}_{1},{b}_{2}}$ and ${{c}_{1},{c}_{2}}$, respectively.\n\nThe change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C$ is a matrix whose columns are the coordinates of vectors from $B$ relative to the basis $C.$\n\nHence, we need to find the coordinates of ${b}_{1}$ and ${b}_{2}$ relative to the basis $C.$ To do this, we consider the matrix\n\n$$\nM=[\\begin{aligned}| & | & | & | \\\\ {c}_{1} & {c}_{2} & {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} \\\\ | & | & | & |\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nWe reduce $M$ using Gaussian elimination until we get the identity matrix on the left. By doing this, we obtain the required change-of-coordinates matrix on the right.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}M & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 8 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-1){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 9 & 3\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}] & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+2{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 & 5 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHence,\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nNow, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-3\\cdot 2}[\\begin{aligned}1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 4 & -5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & -5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 6 \\\\ 2 & -3\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364855 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "61bd0b4f-165c-5f31-b4ec-e0eff7e758e1" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to a Basis Using a Change-of-Coordinates Matrix"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f6f23802-5e26-5b73-bab2-1695814c7e29" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364857} {:answer/id #uuid "17ee376f-cfe4-5d01-8960-02079d97ffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364858} {:answer/id #uuid "5631daa8-3a75-5ef0-bcc7-e2718a47416b" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}12 & -5 \\\\ 26 & -11\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364859} {:answer/id #uuid "34aa0606-03f6-5125-9b72-d7ba219314fd" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364860} {:answer/id #uuid "06ea9253-4abb-5a4d-8a52-41f5687a093d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}10 & -7 \\\\ 24 & -17\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364861}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "17ee376f-cfe4-5d01-8960-02079d97ffac" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364858} :answer-field/id #uuid "1fe2a4d1-d2f3-5886-94a2-6f4af9e7ab64" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364856}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "4f59fe0b-284b-5212-bd9c-6b6ffbb22f39" :question/math-academy-id "q-97116" :question/problem "$$\n[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}],\\,{P}_{B\\to C}=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to the basis $B$ and the change-of-coordinates matrix ${P}_{B\\to C}$ shown above. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the bases $B$ and $C$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/1b81504d6fdcabee0e1f586fef3dd2af5a57a8411ba4d22429e00e09b171a4d1.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{C}={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1}\\,,\n$$\n\nwhere ${P}_{B\\to C}$ is the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $C.$\n\nOur goal is to compute $[T]_{C}.$ First, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to C}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{1\\cdot 5-(-2)\\cdot (-2)}[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the basis $C:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{C} & ={P}_{B\\to C}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to C}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}5 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & -1 \\\\ 4 & -3\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & -1\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}4 & 9 \\\\ -2 & -5\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186364862 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "969220db-a686-511b-b33a-0b4f6ab66b1a" :knowledge-point/title "Finding the Matrix of a Linear Transformation Relative to the Standard Basis"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "29ffd46b-028c-582e-aa34-90a9fa6a81ed" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}11 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364864} {:answer/id #uuid "db64580f-ffed-5f64-a0bc-8c31942cfca9" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-7 & -9 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364865} {:answer/id #uuid "1d8f4b24-e29c-59c9-ba1b-d029ed318afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364866} {:answer/id #uuid "bfc67d3b-2d8a-5d23-be68-058d2131bb66" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}17 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 4\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364867} {:answer/id #uuid "4e9a7bea-9fb0-5ce8-921c-4d859382388d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364868}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1d8f4b24-e29c-59c9-ba1b-d029ed318afe" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364866} :answer-field/id #uuid "5c93c890-81e9-5f10-821c-bf5dd5455496" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186364863}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "521075a2-a304-5840-a75f-21a6502344fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-97120" :question/problem "$$\nB={[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}],[\\begin{aligned}6 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]},\\,[T]_{B}=[\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the basis $B$ of ${R}^{2}$ and the matrix $[T]_{B}$ of a linear transformation $T$ with respect to this basis. Find the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S.$" :question/worked-solution "The connection between the matrices of our linear transformation $T$ relative to the basis $B$ and standard basis $S$ is given by the following diagram:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14070092/assets/dbd020362f950fe8bfb4c592652b9e78a73610fa01a247b0b7ead6f201bf9ea5.png)\n\n \n\nHere, moving along an arrow means pre-multiplying by the corresponding matrix. Algebraically, we can write this as\n\n$$\n[T]_{S}={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1},\n$$\n\nand the change-of-coordinates matrix from $B$ to $S$ is\n\n$$\n{P}_{B\\to S}=[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}].\n$$\n\nFirst, we compute the inverse of ${P}_{B\\to S}:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} & ={[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}]}^{-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{5\\cdot 1-1\\cdot 6}[\\begin{aligned}5 & -6 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we can compute the matrix of $T$ relative to the standard basis $S:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[T]_{S} & ={P}_{B\\to S}\\cdot [T]_{B}\\cdot {P}_{B\\to S}^{-1} \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}1 & 6 \\\\ 1 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-4 & -3 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 9 \\\\ 1 & 7\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}-5 & 6 \\\\ 1 & -1\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}-1 & 3 \\\\ 2 & -1\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
